**Direct answer to Q1:** Yes. `function void print_table(int tbl[string][$]);` is legal. A subroutine argument may declare unpacked dimensions, including associative `[string]` and queue `[$]` dimensions, and VCS 2022.06 compiles and runs it. The typedef form passes an equivalent type. It is still preferred because it names the type once for arguments, class members and function return types, and a return type cannot carry anonymous unpacked dimensions.

**Key idea:** Use typedefs to give a queue and an associative array of queues readable names. `const ref` avoids copying the container and prevents this subroutine from changing it; it is a design choice, not the only legal argument form.

| Type or argument | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| int_queue_t | Queue of int values |
| stream_db_t | String-indexed associative array of queues |
| input stream_db_t | Copy container on entry (O(total elements)); callee writes are lost on return |
| ref stream_db_t | Alias the caller's variable: no copy, callee writes are visible; subroutine must be automatic |
| const ref stream_db_t | Alias without copy, read-only through this argument |

```systemverilog
module queue_arguments;
  typedef int int_queue_t[$];
  typedef int_queue_t stream_db_t[string];
  function automatic void print_streams(const ref stream_db_t db);
    foreach (db[key]) begin
      $display("stream=%s depth=%0d", key, db[key].size());
      if (db[key].size() != 0)
        $display("head=%0d", db[key][0]);
    end
  endfunction
  initial begin
    stream_db_t streams;
    streams["A"] = '{10,20};
    streams["B"] = '{};
    print_streams(streams);
  end
endmodule
```

### Explanation and pitfalls
The two aliases make dimensional ordering explicit. The string dimension selects a stream; the queue dimension selects an element within that stream. Check a queue's size before indexing element zero. Use `exists(key)` when querying an arbitrary key, and decide whether a missing stream differs from an existing empty stream.

A ref argument needs a compatible variable of the expected type; it cannot bind to an arbitrary temporary expression. If queue elements are class handles, copying the container still shares the referenced objects. `const ref` prevents reassignment through the reference, but does not make an entire object graph deeply immutable.

### Follow-up
If the function consumes elements, use mutable `ref` and document the mutation. For asynchronous readers, add a synchronization or snapshot policy; changing an argument declaration does not make concurrent updates safe.

