**Key idea:** `static task` declares a class-scoped method. Class methods have automatic call lifetime; declaring a class method with `task static` lifetime is illegal.

| Declaration inside a class | Legal? | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| static task taskA() | Yes | Call as MyClass::taskA(); automatic call locals |
| task static taskB() | No | Static method lifetime is prohibited |
| static task static taskC() | No | Same prohibited lifetime |
| task taskD() | Yes | Instance method; automatic call locals |

```systemverilog
module method_demo;
  timeunit 1ns; timeprecision 1ps;
  class Worker;
    static task taskA(int id);
      int count = 0;
      count++;
      #10ns;
      $display("id=%0d count=%0d",id,count);
    endtask
  endclass
  initial begin
    fork
      Worker::taskA(1);
      Worker::taskA(2);
    join
  end
endmodule
```

Both calls print count=1 and keep their own id; print ordering is unspecified (verified with VCS 2022.06). No Worker object is needed. A static class method cannot directly access an instance's non-static properties because there is no implicit `this` object.

Legal way to show shared versus per-call storage (VCS 2022.06 output in comments):

```systemverilog
module lifetime_demo;
  timeunit 1ns; timeprecision 1ps;
  class Worker;
    static task taskA(int id);
      static int shared_calls;   // explicit static local: one copy for all calls
      int count = 0;             // automatic local: one copy per call
      shared_calls++; count++;
      #10ns;
      $display("A id=%0d count=%0d shared_calls=%0d", id, count, shared_calls);
    endtask
  endclass
  task static mod_static(int id); // module-scope static task: args and locals shared
    int count;
    count++; #10ns;
    $display("mod id=%0d count=%0d", id, count);
  endtask
  initial begin
    fork Worker::taskA(1); Worker::taskA(2); join  // id=1 count=1 shared_calls=2 / id=2 count=1 shared_calls=2
    fork mod_static(1); mod_static(2); join         // mod id=2 count=2 (printed twice)
  end
endmodule
```

The module-scope static task shows exactly the hazard the question asks about: the second call overwrites the shared `id` argument and both calls increment one `count`. If `task static taskB()` is written inside a class anyway, VCS 2022.06 reports `Warning-[ISLSCM] Illegal static lifetime specified for class method` and still shares the locals. That is tool leniency, not legal code.

### Pitfalls and follow-up
Do not confuse a static class method with a module-scope `task static`, where arguments and locals have shared lifetime. A static class property is also shared across instances and calls, so using one as a counter still requires synchronization if concurrent callers update it. To demonstrate lifetime sharing, use a legal module-scope task or an explicitly static class property, rather than invalid class-method syntax. The illegal declarations in the question are deliberate syntax-analysis cases and should not be compiled as a valid example.

