**Key idea:** Capture each worker's own `process::self()` handle, keep handles local to one invocation, and clean up the losing worker or timer before returning.

| Method | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| status() | Returns FINISHED, RUNNING, WAITING, SUSPENDED, or KILLED |
| kill() | Terminates the selected process and its descendants |
| suspend()/resume() | Suspend or resume that selected process |
| await() | Wait for that process to terminate |

```systemverilog
module watchdog_example;
  timeunit 1ns; timeprecision 1ps;
  task automatic payload(); #50ns; endtask
  task automatic run_with_timeout(input time limit, output bit expired);
    process worker_p;
    bit timeout_hit = 0;
    fork
      begin : isolated_owner
        fork
          begin
            worker_p = process::self();
            payload();
          end
          begin
            wait (worker_p != null);
            #(limit);
            if (worker_p.status() != process::FINISHED) begin
              timeout_hit = 1;
              worker_p.kill();
            end
          end
        join_any
        disable fork;
      end
    join
    expired = timeout_hit;
  endtask
  initial begin
    bit expired;
    run_with_timeout(100ns, expired);
    assert (!expired) else $fatal(1,"unexpected timeout");
    run_with_timeout(10ns, expired);
    assert (expired) else $fatal(1,"missing timeout");
  end
endmodule
```

### Explanation and pitfalls
The nested owner isolates `disable fork` from unrelated children of the caller. Successful completion cancels the timer, so an old timer cannot kill a later transaction. Per-invocation automatic storage also permits concurrent calls without sharing a mutable worker handle. Killing a process does not restore DUT state or release resources; the real payload needs an explicit abort policy.

At an exact deadline, same-time process ordering can affect which branch wins. Specify a tie policy if that boundary matters. A suspended process does not count as successful completion. To retain an independent worker instead of cancelling it, keep its handle and call `await()` from its owner.

