**Key idea:** An exported SV subroutine declared in a module exists once per elaborated instance. A C call such as `sv_poke_reg(...)` does not name an instance, so the simulator resolves it through the *current DPI scope*. A `context` import supplies that scope, which by default is the instance where the import is declared, and only a context import may call exports or use the scope API. To reach another instance, C must look up its scope, switch to it, and restore the previous scope afterwards.

| API | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| svGetScope() | Read the current scope (initially the calling context import's instance) |
| svGetScopeFromName() | Find an elaborated instance by hierarchical name; NULL if not found |
| svSetScope() | Select scope; returns the previous scope so it can be restored |

```systemverilog
module sv_agent #(parameter bit MASTER = 0, parameter string PEER = "");
  export "DPI-C" task sv_poke_reg;
  import "DPI-C" context task c_run_test(input string peer_path);

  task sv_poke_reg(input int addr, input int data);
    #1;  // an exported task may consume time
    $display("%m t=%0t addr=%h data=%0d", $time, addr, data);
  endtask

  initial if (MASTER) c_run_test(PEER);
endmodule

module tb;
  sv_agent #(.MASTER(1), .PEER("tb.u1")) u0();
  sv_agent                                u1();
endmodule
```

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "svdpi.h"

/* C prototype from IEEE 1800-2017 35.9: an exported SV task returns int
   (1 = disabled, 0 = normal return). */
extern int sv_poke_reg(int addr, int data);

int c_run_test(const char *peer_path)
{
    svScope caller = svGetScope();          /* instance that called the import */
    svScope peer   = svGetScopeFromName(peer_path);
    svScope prev;
    int disabled;

    if (caller == NULL || peer == NULL) {
        printf("c_run_test: unknown scope %s\n", peer_path);
        return 0;
    }
    disabled = sv_poke_reg(0x1000, 1);      /* resolves in the caller instance */
    if (disabled) return 1;                 /* propagate disable acknowledgment */

    prev = svSetScope(peer);                /* switch to the other instance */
    disabled = sv_poke_reg(0x2000, 2);
    svSetScope(prev);                       /* restore before continuing */
    if (disabled) return 1;

    return sv_poke_reg(0x3000, 3);          /* back in the caller instance */
}
```

Verified with VCS 2022.06 (`vcs -full64 -sverilog tb.sv dpi.c -top tb`). The output is `tb.u0.sv_poke_reg t=1 ...`, then `tb.u1.sv_poke_reg t=2 ...`, then `tb.u0.sv_poke_reg t=3 ...`. The same C call reaches different instances only because of the scope switch.

### Explanation
- **Why context:** with plain `import "DPI-C" task c_run_test`, VCS 2022.06 compiles the code but fails at run time with `Error-[DPI-INCDIC] ... A time consuming export DPI task can only be invoked from a context DPI task`.
- **Why scope management:** `tb.u0` and `tb.u1` each own a `sv_poke_reg`. Without `svSetScope`, every call from this import lands in `tb.u0`. Saving and restoring the previous scope keeps later code in the import from silently running in the wrong instance.
- **C signatures:** IEEE 1800-2017 §35.9 gives imported and exported *tasks* an `int` return on the C side (the disable protocol). Generated headers differ between tools. VCS 2022.06's `vc_hdrs.h` declares both tasks above as `void`, so including it next to these `int` definitions is a C error (`conflicting types for 'c_run_test'`). On VCS, either match `vc_hdrs.h` or omit it. On other simulators, compile against their generated header.

### Pitfalls and follow-ups
A non-context import must not call exports or scope-dependent APIs. A pure import has stronger side-effect restrictions. An imported *function* cannot call an exported *task*, because a function cannot consume time. Calling an export from an arbitrary OS thread or signal handler is illegal even after `svSetScope`. Enter SV only through the simulator's own call chain. If an exported task returns 1 (disabled), the import must return 1 promptly.

