**Key idea:** A shuffled bag gives a permutation of a small finite domain, consumed one value at a time. It is a separate generator API, not a replacement for the constraint solver's `randomize()` semantics.

| WIDTH=2 example cycle | Outputs |
| --- | --- |
| First shuffle | 2, 0, 3, 1 |
| Second shuffle | 1, 3, 0, 2 |

Repeating a value at a cycle boundary is legal. Only repeats within a cycle are forbidden.

```systemverilog
class manual_cycle #(parameter int WIDTH = 3);
  typedef bit [WIDTH-1:0] value_t;
  protected value_t bag[$];
  function new();
    if (WIDTH < 1 || WIDTH > 16)
      $fatal(1,"Example supports WIDTH 1 through 16");
  endfunction
  function value_t get_next();
    if (bag.size() == 0) begin
      for (int i=0; i<(1<<WIDTH); i++) bag.push_back(value_t'(i));
      for (int i=bag.size()-1; i>0; i--) begin
        int j = $urandom_range(i,0);
        value_t tmp = bag[i];
        bag[i] = bag[j];
        bag[j] = tmp;
      end
    end
    return bag.pop_back();
  endfunction
endclass
```

### Explanation and pitfalls
Fisher-Yates chooses one of the remaining positions on each iteration. Construction and refill need O(2^WIDTH) time and storage, so the example deliberately bounds the width. Each subsequent pop is constant work. Seed and call ordering determine reproducibility.

Built-in randc can participate in constraints, with limitations. IEEE 1800-2017 §18.4.2 lets an implementation cap the size of a randc variable, but the cap may not be below 8 bits. Check the selected simulator's actual limit. Overwriting a rand property in post_randomize can invalidate solved constraints; this class therefore exposes only get_next. It does not promise to satisfy arbitrary relational constraints or adapt its cycle to changing legal-value sets.

### Follow-up
To support a fixed sparse legal domain, fill the bag with those values. For changing constraints, define whether changing the legal set resets the cycle, and use solver-based exclusion with explicit unsatisfiable handling where appropriate.

